# **EXPRESSIONALISM**

*A short account of what shows up when you look*

*If anything “is” — whatever it is — what else (beyond its own inclusion) must be certain?*

*This is not the answer.*

by

Witten

# **Introduction**

*The world is ordinary and already a little strange*

You drive. You make coffee. You walk to work. You blink. You sleep. You pay a tax. The same world also holds things that are not a day in a life: a star, the moon in a telescope, two plus two, a prayer, a rumor of God, a rumor that this is a simulation, a dream. Same world. Ordinary. Already a little odd if you linger.

Most of the time nothing extra is required. The cup is for drinking. The car is for going. That way of being with things can stay as it is. This book does not turn living into homework.

Sometimes a look starts. It is not always a large question. Sometimes it is only: what is this, really? You are no longer only living with it.

People use different words for that moment. Knowing. Analyzing. Assessing. Being certain. Different words leaning at the same wall. This book will say *look*. Then it will stop announcing the choice.

This book will not decide whether living is secretly always looking. It will not decide whether there is a person, a brain that never stops, a reader, a consciousness. It does not know those things and does not need them.

What it is interested in is smaller. Once a look has started, some things seem to be the case. Part 1 names them. Part 2 lets you run them on one *this*, with a pen. Then you can close the book.

If numbers show up later, they are marks on this look. They are not the cup, the sum, or the meaning. A moral claim, a proof, a “how do I know,” a “what is it like” can each be a this. The book is not an ethics, a logic, a theory of knowledge, or a study of experience. It will look at those the way it looks at a cup.

Someone will say this is circular. That it creates objects. That it proves a hidden outside. That anything goes. That it says nothing. Those get answered where they come up, in a few sentences. Not in a pile at the front.

This short account is called Expressionalism. A thicker book exists under the same name if you want the machinery. You do not have to go there.

# **Part 1\. What seems to be the case**

Once a look has started, some things seem to hold. The sections below name them. They belong to one look, written in pieces so they can be read one at a time.

## **Once a look has started**

If a look has started, you do not first have to decide what the world is made of. Matter, mind, God, a simulation, turtles all the way down — any of those might be true, or none of them. Almost anything could be going on, including nothing you could use. The look still starts on something already here. A cup on the table. A number on a page.

That open space is not a picture of the universe. It is only this: the look has not first picked a world-picture. There is no guarantee that the world is tidy, or that it will hold still. The look proceeds anyway, without that promise.

You can look at any of those world-pictures later, as a this of its own. You do not have to pick one in order to look at the cup.

*The look starts in the middle. You have not first chosen what the world is made of. The cup is already on the table.*

Someone will say: then you are only describing looking, so you are saying nothing. What it seems to be saying is what a look requires, and what a look cannot take in.

## **This against that**

If there is a look, there has to be contrast. A this only shows as this because something else is not it. Sometimes the contrast is sitting in the same scene. The heat of the coffee against the word cup is like that. Sometimes what came forward will not join the look at all. Steam rising off the coffee is like that, if all you wrote down was that there is a cup on the table.

You do not need a country called Non-Existence for contrast to work. “Not this” is enough.

## **What holds**

Once there is difference, some of it connects. A cup on the table is held up by a shape you can hold, by the word cup, by heat if there is coffee, by drinking, by other cups you have used. Some of those point back to the this you named. Some stop short. Steam does not join this look.

Leftover of this look is what came forward and does not hold this up, this time. It is not a defect. It is not a hidden substance sitting in the ceramic.

The supports are not the cup. Putting them together in this look is what makes it the examined cup. Change that putting-together and you have not smashed the ceramic. You have changed the look.

Someone will say: so looking made the cup.

No. Living already had a cup. The look makes a cup-under-a-look.

Someone will say: so there is a mysterious outside.

No realm was found. If you look at “the outside,” it enters the this and is no longer outside. You just drew a bigger box. The rim is still there: this look is not the world.

## **Jobs**

In the look, something is being held and something is doing the holding. Those are jobs in this look. They are not labels glued to the furniture.

Next look, the word cup can sit in the middle, and this object on the table can be one of the things holding it. Same world. Different job.

## **Opening a support**

What holds the cup is built the same way. Heat, if you open it, is held by a sensation, a word for warmth, a burned mouth. The same picture can happen inside a support.

You can keep opening. You do not have to settle whether it is supports all the way down, or turtles, before you look at the cup. Nesting is allowed. It is not required. You can drink instead.

## **The thing and the others**

There is a current thing in the middle, and others sitting with it. Until both are there, you only have a scene and some differences in the scene. Once both are there, you can ask what is holding this up.

## **Putting them together**

Some of what came forward lines up with the this you wrote down. Some of it does not. The shape you can hold may feel more like the cup you named. A factory rumor, if it came forward at all, may feel less like it. Steam may sit nearby and still refuse to hold the sentence up. Noticing that much is already a kind of measuring.

The noticing is a reading of this look. It is not a verdict on the world. It is not an average sitting on the page.

Someone will say: who decides, so anything goes. For this look, what holds is what points back to the this you wrote down. Write it down another way and you get a different cast. No referee sits above two boxes. You pick the marks. Two honest looks can disagree.

## **You can stop**

You can keep looking. You can change a support. You can look at the looking. Or you can go drink. The book does not follow you home.

# **Part 2\. A page you can use**

If Part 1 is right, a look can be run on one this. A pen is enough. A machine is not required. This is not a new method. It is the same look, written down.

The first half of this part shows how the page works. The next page is blank, so you can copy it or tear it out. After that, the same page already filled in, so the blanks have a body.

## **What this look is looking at**

Write down what this look is looking at. That writing is the box. The box does not have to be a sentence.

A this can be a word, a sentence, a paragraph, a picture, a dance, a clip, a book. It can be an idea, an object, a feeling, an experience, a sensation, an intuition. When this book needs one word that can cover all of those, it will say expression. If this or thing sits better in your hand, use that.

You write it down so the page has a middle. You have not turned a dance into a sentence. You have named the box.

“There is a cup on the table” is one look. “There is a cup on the table because we scored” is a different look. Same cup. Different box.

What comes forward is asked to point back at what you wrote.

Strong means it points back. It does not mean the cup is more of a cup. Weak means it barely does. Some sits between. None means it came forward and does not hold this up. That is leftover of this look. It is not leftover-stuff sitting in the cup.

Four or five things that came forward are enough. What did not come forward stays off the page. That is unlisted. It is not leftover, and it is not the rim. Do not hunt it. Hunting it turns living into homework.

The rim is one line: this look is not the world.

Someone will say leftover is a hidden something. No. Leftover is what you listed that does not hold this up, this time. Someone will say looking made the cup. No. Living already had a cup. The look makes a cup-under-a-look.

## **Four names that do not trade places**

| Holders | Listed things that point back. |
| :---- | :---- |
| **Leftover of this look** | Listed things that do not hold this up. |
| **Unlisted** | What never came forward. Off the sheet. Do not hunt it. |
| **Rim** | This look is not the world. One line. |

Tension is seeing the two piles already on the page — holders and leftover. It is not a number under them.

## **The short card**

If you want the whole method in four lines:

|   1   What is this? |
| :---- |
|   2   What is holding it up? |
|   3   What came forward and does not hold? |
|   4   This look is not the world. |

Then the exit, which is not the rim: look again, or go live? If that is all that gets used, that is enough. The longer page is the same four names, slower.

## **How to fill the page**

Title the page with what you are looking at. Write it again so it is sitting there. List what came forward. Four or five is enough.

Mark each one none, weak, some, or strong. If this look wants numbers, use 0, 1, 2, or 3\. Those are the same four rungs written as digits. Use one ladder on a line, not both.

Now look only at the ones that still point back — the ones you marked weak, some, or strong. Leave the ones marked none to the leftover pile. They have a different job.

Among the ones that still hold, do they sit together as one picture of what you wrote down, or do two of them clash? They can be unlike and still hold. If two holders clash, circle those two and leave it at that. A clash between holders is not leftover. Leftover is the pile that did not hold.

Write the rim as one line. Do not make a third leftover list there.

Someone has to pick the marks. This page will say you, because you are the one holding the pen. If it helps to call yourself a person, do that. This book does not know what a person is, and it does not need to. The marks belong to this look. Two honest looks can disagree. The sheet is not a judge.

## **How to read what you wrote**

When the page is full, look at it. One pile is holding this up. The other pile came forward and did not join. See both at once. That seeing is the tension. Do not mash “how many held” and “how many did not” into one score and treat that number as what the cup means.

If you used the numbers, you can count how many listed things held and how many did not. Those two counts can sit next to each other. They are still two facts. They are not a score for the cup, the sum, or the sky. Then look again with a rewritten this, or go live.

